      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "from scipy import stats\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Section 2.1.1 Modules for Descriptive Statistics"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "rVKcNS-8k718"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Given a vector of synthetic values, the modules commonly used for descriptive statistics can be introduced. This can be made reproducible by fixing the seed.\n",
        "In Python, it is implemented by creating a NumPy random number generator with a fixed seed and drawing from a normal distribution.\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "2_WMOBVlk6e7"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "# Reproducible random generator (seed = 2340)\n",
        "rng = np.random.default_rng(2340)\n",
        "\n",
        "x = rng.normal(loc=1.5, scale=1.0, size=(100))"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Ald1EX0diuYi"
      },
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Python equivalents (NumPy / SciPy) for the summary statistics\n",
        "\n",,
        "NumPy covers the most common descriptive statistics directly for `ndarray` data with consistent handling of shapes and axes. For statistics, such as skewness and robust measures like median absolute deviation (MAD), it is common to use `scipy.stats`. This module contains a large number of  summary and frequency statistics, correlation functions, and statistical tests, as well as probability distributions.\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Notes\n",
        "- **NaN handling:** `np.nanmean`, `np.nanmedian`, `np.nanstd`, `np.nanvar` versions handle missing values in place. For SciPy versions, filtering `NaN`s first is required.\n",
        "- **Standard deviation / variance:** MATLAB defaults correspond to **sample** estimates. In NumPy, this is achieved with `ddof=1`.\n",
        "- **MAD:** `scale=1.0` ensures the *raw* MAD (no normalization), matching MATLAB `mad(x,1)`.\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "HMvDFDV1qKHe"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "# Descriptive statistics (MATLAB equivalents)\n",
        "mean_x   = np.mean(x)\n",
        "median_x = np.median(x)\n",
        "std_x    = np.std(x, ddof=1)          # MATLAB std(x) default (N-1)\n",
        "var_x    = np.var(x, ddof=1)          # MATLAB var(x) default (N-1)\n",
        "\n",
        "# MATLAB skewness(x,1) (biased moment skewness)\n",
        "skew_x   = stats.skew(x)\n",
        "\n",
        "range_x  = np.ptp(x)                  # max(x) - min(x)\n",
        "\n",
        "q_1_4    = np.quantile(x, 1/4)        # quantile(x,1/4)\n",
        "q_3_5    = np.quantile(x, 3/5)        # quantile(x,3/5)\n",
        "\n",
        "mad_x    = stats.median_abs_deviation(x, scale=1.0)  # mad(x,1)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Output (simple printout)\n",
        "print(f\"mean(x)      = {mean_x:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"median(x)    = {median_x:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"std(x)       = {std_x:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"var(x)       = {var_x:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"std(x)^2     = {std_x**2:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"skewness(x)  = {skew_x:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"range(x)     = {range_x:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"quantile(x,1/4) = {q_1_4:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"quantile(x,3/5) = {q_3_5:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"mad(x,1)     = {mad_x:.4f}\")\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "psKyNK7gowmQ",
        "outputId": "ba486b11-31c4-48a0-c482-555cb5a31e6f"
      },
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "mean(x)      = 1.5298\n",
            "median(x)    = 1.6086\n",
            "std(x)       = 0.9812\n",
            "var(x)       = 0.9628\n",
            "std(x)^2     = 0.9628\n",
            "skewness(x)  = 0.1418\n",
            "range(x)     = 5.8467\n",
            "quantile(x,1/4) = 0.8126\n",
            "quantile(x,3/5) = 1.7921\n",
            "mad(x,1)     = 0.6384\n"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
