    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Normal probability plot with `scipy.stats.probplot`\n",
        "\n",
        "Matplotlib does not provide a dedicated method for that due to the requirement for  a statistical preprocessing step. To build the plot, the data sample must be sorted and compared to the theoretical quantiles of a chosen distribution. In Python, the suitable method for that is `scipy.stats.probplot`, while Matplotlib can be used to display the result.\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "krgxzzOTkAc4"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "# 0) Creating a reproducible synthetic sample\n",
        "rng = np.random.default_rng(2340)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Example A: approximately normal data (should align well with the reference line)\n",
        "x = rng.normal(loc=1.5, scale=1.0, size=(100, 1))\n",
        "\n",
        "# 1) Ensuring the shape of a sample to be a 1D NumPy array (useful if x was created as shape (n,1))\n",
        "x = np.asarray(x, dtype=float).ravel()\n",
        "\n",
        "# 2) (Optional) Removing NaNs\n",
        "x = x[~np.isnan(x)]\n",
        "\n",
        "# 3) Computing plot data for a Normal distribution\n",
        "(osm, osr), (slope, intercept, r) = stats.probplot(x, dist=\"norm\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# 4) Creating a new figure\n",
        "plt.figure()\n",
        "\n",
        "# 5) Plotting the points (ordered sample vs theoretical quantiles)\n",
        "plt.plot(osm, osr, \"o\", markerfacecolor=\"none\")  # open circles\n",
        "\n",
        "# 6) Creating endpoints for the fitted reference line (covering the osm range)\n",
        "x_line = np.array([osm.min(), osm.max()])\n",
        "y_line = slope * x_line + intercept\n",
        "\n",
        "# 7) Plotting the fitted reference line\n",
        "plt.plot(x_line, y_line, \"-k\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# 8) Adding plot annotations (title, labels, grid)\n",
        "plt.title(\"Normal Probability Plot (Q–Q plot)\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Theoretical Quantiles (Normal)\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Ordered Sample Values\")\n",
        "plt.grid(True)\n",
        "\n",
        "# 9) Displaying the plot\n",
        "plt.show()\n",
        "\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
